:::: {.column width=45%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L08.4.Simpson_Paradox.png)

::::
:::

* Simpson's Paradox: Admission Bias in University

- University has male acceptance 67.5% and female 48.3%

- @Question@: is there gender bias against females?

- @Solution@: stratify by department

  ```{=typst}
  #styled-table(
    headers: ("Dept", "M Apps", "M Accept", "M Rate", "F Apps", "F Accept", "F Rate"),
    rows: (
      ("Overall", "120", "81", "67.5%", "120", "58", "48.3%"),
      ("Engineering", "100", "80", "80%", "20", "18", "90%"),
      ("English", "20", "1", "5%", "100", "40", "40%"),
    ),
    col-widths: (1fr, 0.8fr, 0.9fr, 0.8fr, 0.8fr, 0.9fr, 0.8fr),
  )
  ```

- Within each dept, women have higher acceptance
  - Engineering: 90% (F) vs 80% (M)
  - English: 40% (F) vs 5% (M)

- @Key insight@: women apply more to harder dept
  - Engineering (high acceptance) attracts more men
  - English (low acceptance) attracts more women
  - Aggregation masks this pattern

// Identifying the Treatment Effect

* Identifying the Treatment Effect
- @Question@: 
  - We can't observe $\EE[Y_{1i} - Y_{0i}]$ since we can only observe one of the
    potential outcomes $Y_{1i}$ or $Y_{0i}$, but _not both_
  - How can we recover causal quantities from observable data?

- @Solution@: the bias equation says that:
  \begingroup
  \small
  $$
  \underbrace{\EE[Y | T = 1] - \EE[Y | T = 0]} =
  \underbrace{\EE[Y_1 - Y_0 | T = 1]}_{\text{ATT}} +
  \underbrace{\EE[Y_0 | T = 1] - \EE[Y_0 | T = 0]}_{\text{Bias}}
  $$
  \endgroup

- **Case 1**: If the treatment and the control group are comparable regardless of
  the treatment (exchangeable), i.e., $\EE[Y_0|T=0] = \EE[Y_0|T=1]$ then:
  - The bias term vanishes: $bias = 0$
  - The measured difference in means equals $ATT = \EE[(Y_1 - Y_0) | T = 1]$

- **Case 2**: If treatment effect is homogeneous (both groups respond similarly
  to treatment), i.e., $\EE[(Y_1 - Y_0) | T = 1] = \EE[(Y_1 - Y_0) | T = 0]$
  then:
  - The measured difference of means equals $ATE = \EE[Y_1 - Y_0]$

- @Example@: if businesses that cut and don't cut prices are similar to each
  other (i.e., exchangeable), then the difference in amount sold be entirely
  attributed to the price cut

* Independence Property (Aka Unconfoundedness)
- @Definition@: the independence property means that _potential outcomes_ are
  independent of the treatment:
  $$
  (Y_0, Y_1) \perp T
  $$

- It's usually stated conditional on covariates: $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T | X$
  - Without $X$ it implies complete randomization (like a perfect experiment)
  - In observational settings, we almost always need conditioning

- @Implication@
  - $\EE[Y_0 | T] = \EE[Y_0]$: the treatment gives no information about potential
    outcomes
  - $\EE[Y_0 | T=1] = \EE[Y_0 | T=0]$: both groups have the same $Y_0$ in average
    (exchangeable)

* Independence Property (Aka Unconfoundedness)
- In $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T$
  - We are not asking for _observed outcome_ $Y$ and treatment $T$ to be
    independent (i.e., the treatment has no effect)
  - We are asking for each _potential outcome_ $Y_0$ and $Y_1$ to be independent
    of the treatment
  - _"Knowing whether someone received the treatment tells you nothing about what
    their outcome would have been without it"_
  - Treatment assignment is as good as random, so differences in outcomes can be
    attributed to the treatment

- @Example@: Sales
  - We can't tell apart businesses that chose to engage in sales from those that
    didn't

* Phases of Causal Inference
- @Algorithm@: A causal inference problem is often broken down into two steps:
  1. **Identification**
     - Express the causal quantity of interest in terms of observable data
     - Remove the bias, making treated and untreated comparable, so that all
       the difference can be attributed to the treatment effect
       $$\EE[Y_0 | T=0] = \EE[Y_0 | T=1]$$
     - You need to know how the treatment was distributed or assigned
     - This is why data alone (observational) cannot answer causal questions
  2. **Estimation**
     - Use data to estimate the causal quantity of interest:
       $$\EE[Y | T=1] - \EE[Y | T=0]$$

* Randomization Removes Bias
- Randomization of treatment forces independence to hold $\implies$ removes bias
  $$\EE[Y | T = 1] - \EE[Y | T = 0] = \EE[Y_1 - Y_0]$$
  - The measured difference of means is the true ATE (Average Treatment Effect)

- @Example@: Sales
  - Randomize the treatment, i.e., by forcing a business to cut prices
    $$
    \left\{
    \begin{aligned}
    & IsOnSales \leftarrow rand(t) \\
    & AmountSold \leftarrow f_y(IsOnSales, u_y) \\
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
    $$
  - The dependency from $u_t$ is replaced by assignment mechanism that is:
    - Tied to a coin flip (unrelated to other factors in causal mechanism)
    - Fully known

- @Caveat@
  - Randomizing eligibility doesn't mean randomizing participation
  - E.g., online retailer randomly offered membership program
  - But only engaged customers opted in, so self-selection remained

// Chap 2: Randomized Experiments and Stats Review

# Randomized Experiments and Stats Review

* A/B Testing: Example

- @Fact@: a common company strategy is:
  - Free/cheap product attracts customers and get customers' emails
  - Cross-sell premium via email campaigns
  - "Converted" = customer buys premium

- @Fact@: in general non-randomized data show emails boost conversion
  $$
  \EE[Conversion | Email = 1] > \EE[Conversion | Email = 0]
  $$

- @Problem@: selection bias in observational data
  - Marketing team tends to send emails for customers who they think they will
    convert (e.g., using a survey, customers who visited the website the most)
  - Those customers would convert anyway:
    $$
    \EE[Conversion_0 | Email = 1] > \EE[Conversion_0 | Email = 0]
    $$

- @Solution@: randomize email assignment via A/B test
  - Makes groups comparable, isolates treatment effect

* A/B Testing: Example
- @Example@: you have 3 type of emails:
  - No email (`no_email`)
  - Long and beautiful email (`long`)
  - Short and to-the-point email (`short`)

- Collect the data (`id`, `gender`, `age`, `cross_sell_email`, `conversion`)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Compute the mean by group in `cross_sell_email`

- Compute the ATE, i.e., difference between each treated group and control
  $$ATE = \EE[Y | T=t] - \EE[Y | T=0]$$
  - Sending the short and to-the-point email is best
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

\begingroup
\small
| cross_sell_email | conversion |
|------------------|------------|
| no_email         | 0.042      |
| long             | 0.055      |
| short            | 0.125      |
\endgroup

::::
:::

- @Caveat@: sanity check that randomization was done right
  - E.g., check if `gender` and `age` are balanced in the groups using a
    statistical test
  - If not, might be effect is due to sending the email to a specific demographic
  - Biases sampling has large effect on small data

* Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs)
- @Fact@: RCTs are the most reliable way to measure causal effects (gold
  standard)

- @Problems@
  - Very expensive (in time and money)
  - Potentially unethical
  - Sometimes impossible (e.g., selection bias)

- RCT can be used as a benchmark
  - _"What is the best experiment to answer your question (even if it's not)
    feasible?"_
  - Can help reasoning about the causal effect

* Small Size effect
- @Definition@: When the data is small, it's more likely to have extremes just
  by chance
  - Standard error of the mean $SE = \frac{\sigma}{n}$ is related to the sample
    size $n$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Example@
  - You have data about schools in terms of number of students and average SAT
    score
  - Often top-performing schools have a small number of students
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

// TODO: Improve fig layout
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L08.4.School_score_by_number_students.png)

::::
:::

- @Naive answer@
  - A possible (but potentially wrong) explanation is that _"fewer students
    allows teachers to give focused attention to each student"_
  - _Counterpoint_: also bottom 1% of schools have few students

- @Answer@
  - The effect is due to standard error of the mean: more students means more
    precise estimate and less variation due to chance
  - This is shown by a "triangular" shape of `avg_score` vs
    `number_of_students`

// Chap 3: Graphical Causal Models

# Graphical Causal Models

* Graphical Models
- @Definition@: **Graphical Causal Models** are DAGs
  - Arrows show causal direction
    - Causality is non-reversible
  - Missing edges means no direct causal link

- @Pros@
  - Make causal assumptions explicit (and visual)
  - Enable communication of causal thinking
  - Clarify complex relationships

- @Definition@: **Structural Causal Models** (SCMs) use both graphs and causal
  equations

* Email Conversion Example
- @Goal@: Estimate impact of emails on conversion
  - Treatment $T$ = cross-sell email
  - Outcome $Y$ = customer converted to a new product (or not)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}

- @Assumption@: $T$ was randomized, so:
  - The treatment is independent from potential outcomes
    $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T$
  - Association equals causation
    $\EE[Y | T=1] - \EE[Y | T=0] = \EE[Y_1 - Y_0] = ATE$

- You believe that several other variables cause conversion
  - `age`, `gender`
  - Unobserved variables $U$ (e.g., customer income, city we are operating
    in)

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph CausalKG {
  rankdir=LR;

  u [shape=ellipse, style="filled,dashed", color="#183B4A", fillcolor="#E6EEF1", fontcolor="#183B4A", label="u"];

  age [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", color="#62D4A4", fillcolor="#EAF9F3", fontcolor="black", label="age"];
  gender [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", color="#62D4A4", fillcolor="#EAF9F3", fontcolor="black", label="gender"];

  rnd [shape=ellipse, penwidth=2, style="filled,solid", color="#408AB0", fillcolor="#EAF3F8", fontcolor="black", label="rnd"];

  cross_sell_email [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", color="#62D4A4", fillcolor="#EAF9F3", fontcolor="black", label="cross_sell_email"];

  conversion [shape=box, penwidth=2, style="filled,solid", color="#F8D476", fillcolor="#FFF6DA", fontcolor="black", label="conversion"];

  u -> age [style=solid, color=black];
  u -> gender [style=solid, color=black];
  age -> conversion [style=solid, color=black];
  gender -> conversion [style=solid, color=black];
  rnd -> cross_sell_email [style=solid, color=black];
  cross_sell_email -> conversion [style=solid, color=black];
  u -> conversion [style=solid, color=black];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Key insight@: You can't infer the independence assumption from the data
  - You need information about treatment mechanism (e.g., _"emails were
    randomized"_)

* Example: Are Consultants Worth It?

- @Example@: You are a manager and you need to decide whether to hire expensive
  consultants

- @Question@: Which statement is true?
  - Do expensive consultants improve business?
  - Only very profitable businesses can afford consultants?

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Solution@
  - Setting up a randomized trial is difficult
  - You need to untangle causation from association
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph CausalGraph {
  rankdir=LR;

  // Exogenous variable: ProfitsPrev6mos
  ProfitsPrev6mos [
    shape=ellipse,
    penwidth=2,
    color="#408AB0",
    fillcolor="#EAF3F8",
    style="filled,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="ProfitsPrev6mos"
  ];

  // Endogenous variable: Consultancy
  Consultancy [
    shape=box,
    penwidth=1,
    color="#62D4A4",
    fillcolor="#EAF9F3",
    style="filled,rounded,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="Consultancy"
  ];

  // Target variable: ProfitsNext6mos
  ProfitsNext6mos [
    shape=box,
    penwidth=2,
    color="#F8D476",
    fillcolor="#FFF6DA",
    style="filled,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="ProfitsNext6mos"
  ];

  // Causal edges
  ProfitsPrev6mos -> Consultancy [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
  ProfitsPrev6mos -> ProfitsNext6mos [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
  Consultancy -> ProfitsNext6mos [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
}
```

::::
:::

// TODO(gp): Merge in Lesson08.2

* Chains
- @Definition@: A **chain** is a causal graph $T \to M \to Y$:
  - $T$ is the _treatment_
  - $Y$ is the _outcome_
  - $M$ is the _"mediator"_ since it mediates the relationship between $T$ and
    $Y$

- @Key insight@:
  - Causation flows only in the direction of the arrows
    - I.e., changing $M$ affects $Y$, but doesn't affect $T$
  - Association flows both ways
    - I.e., $T$ and $Y$ are dependent, $T \not\perp Y$

- @Proposition@: conditioning on mediator variable blocks association flow
  $$(T \perp Y) | M$$
  - The path is blocked

* Chains: Example
- Consider $Study \to SolveProblems \to JobPromotion$

- $JobPromotion$ is **causally dependent** on $Study$

- $Study$ and $JobPromotion$ are **associated** (the association goes both ways):
  - The more you study, the more likely to get a promotion
  - The greater the chances of promotion, the greater your chance of having
    studied (otherwise it would be difficult to get a promotion)
  - So $Study$ and $JobPromotion$ are:
    - Dependent, i.e., not independent $Study \not\perp JobPromotion$

- **Conditioning on $M$** (i.e., if you hold the intermediary variable fixed),
  which means _"looking for only people with the same skills at problem solving"_
  - The association and dependency is blocked
  - $T$ and $Y$ are conditionally independent given $M$, i.e., $(T \perp Y) | M$
  - $\EE[Y | T, M] = \EE[Y | M]$
  - Also the inverse $\EE[T | Y, M] = \EE[T | M]$

* Forks
- @Definition@: a **fork** is a causal graph $T \leftarrow X \rightarrow Y$:
  - $X$ is a _"confounder"_ (common cause of $T$ and $Y$)

- @Example@: $AnswerQuestion \leftarrow GoodProgrammer \rightarrow GoodEmployee$
  - For this reason in tech interviews you are asked to solve problems that you
    might never find in the job you apply for (e.g., "invert a binary tree")

- @Proposition@: $T$ and $Y$ are dependent $T \not\perp Y$, even though neither
  causes the other
  - Association flows backward through the arrows

- @Proposition@: conditioning on the common cause blocks the association:
  $$(T \perp Y) | X$$

- @Example@: if I already know that you are a good programmer (e.g., you have an
  impressive resume), asking you a tech question won't give me new information
  about whether you are a good employee or not

* An inverted fork
- @Definition@: an **inverted fork** is a causal graph $T \rightarrow X \leftarrow Y$:
  - $X$ is a _"collider"_ (two arrows collide at it)
  - $T$ and $Y$ are independent: $T \perp Y$

- @Proposition@: conditioning on $X$ makes $T$ and $Y$ dependent:
  $T \not\perp Y | X$ and "opens the path" between $T$ and $Y$
  - This is also called _"explaining away"_: one cause explains the effect,
    making the other cause less likely
  - The path is open if you condition on any descendant of the collider

- @Example@: $GoodAtMachineLearning \rightarrow Promotion \leftarrow FlatterBoss$
  - There are two ways to get a job promotion
    - $GoodAtMachineLearning$: You can be good at machine learning
    - $FlatterBoss$ You can flatter your boss
  - If I don't know if you got or not the promotion (i.e., I don't condition on
    $Promotion$) your level of ML and flattering are independent
  - If I know you got promoted, and I know you are _bad at ML_, then you must be
    good at flattering your boss

// TODO(ai_gp): Move
* D-separation
- @Theorem@: a path is **blocked** given a conditioning set $\mathcal{Z}$ if and
  only if either condition holds:
  1. $\mathcal{Z}$ contains a non-collider that has been conditioned on
  2. $\mathcal{Z}$ contains a collider that has not been conditioned on and has
     no descendants that have been conditioned on

- Otherwise the path is **open** (i.e., nodes can be dependent)

- @Theorem@: two nodes $A$ and $B$ are **d-separated** (conditionally
  independent) given $\mathcal{Z}$ if and only if all the paths between them are
  blocked

// Identification Revisited

* Identification
- @Definition@: **identification** is the process of isolating causal
  relationships between treatment $T$ and outcome $Y$

- @Algorithm@
- Using causal graphs:
  - Understand where the bias is coming from and thus how to neutralize it
  - Identification phase becomes equivalent to "close all undesirable flows of
    association"

- @Theorem@: After all sources of (non-causal) association are closed, the
  treatment behaves as if it was "randomized"

* Identification: Consultancy Example
::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}
- Consider the consultancy graph
::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

// TODO(ai_gp): Highlight the backdoor path and the confounder

```graphviz
digraph CausalGraph {
  rankdir=LR;

  // Exogenous variable: ProfitsPrev6mos
  ProfitsPrev6mos [
    shape=ellipse,
    penwidth=2,
    color="#408AB0",
    fillcolor="#EAF3F8",
    style="filled,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="ProfitsPrev6mos"
  ];

  // Endogenous variable: Consultancy
  Consultancy [
    shape=box,
    penwidth=1,
    color="#62D4A4",
    fillcolor="#EAF9F3",
    style="filled,rounded,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="Consultancy"
  ];

  // Target variable: ProfitsNext6mos
  ProfitsNext6mos [
    shape=box,
    penwidth=2,
    color="#F8D476",
    fillcolor="#FFF6DA",
    style="filled,solid",
    fontcolor=black,
    label="ProfitsNext6mos"
  ];

  // Causal edges
  ProfitsPrev6mos -> Consultancy [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
  ProfitsPrev6mos -> ProfitsNext6mos [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
  Consultancy -> ProfitsNext6mos [style=solid, dir=forward, color=black];
}
```
::::
:::

- The graph has:
  - An inverted fork (collider) on $ProfitsNext6mos$
  - A fork (confounder) on $ProfitsPrev6mos$
  - A causal path $consultancy \to ProfitsNext6mos$
  - A non-causal path (aka "backdoor path")
    $ProfitsPrev6mos \to ProfitsNext6mos$

- To close the non-causal flows of association you need to condition on the
  confounder, i.e., $ProfitsPrev6mos$
  - Look at a group of companies with similar past performance
  - Compare the ones that hired consultants (treated) vs the ones who didn't
    (untreated)
  - You are "imposing" conditional independence, i.e., $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T | X$,
    $ProfitNext6mos \perp Consultancy | ProfitsPrev6mos$

* Backdoor Adjustment
- @Definition@: **backdoor adjustment** means closing backdoor paths by
  conditioning on confounders $X$ to make the causal quantity identifiable $Y$
  from observable data

- You are inducing the "conditional independence assumption", i.e.,
  $(Y_0, Y_1) \perp T | X$
  - I.e., units with the same level of covariates $X$ have potential outcomes
    that are the same on average (it's as if they were randomized)

- You can then use the **adjustment formula**
  \begingroup
  \small
  $$
  \begin{aligned}
    ATE
      &= \EE_X\big[ \EE[Y | T=1] - \EE[Y | T=0] \big] \\
      &= \sum_x \big( \EE[Y | T=1, X=x] - \EE[Y | T=0, X=x] \big) \Pr(X=x)
  \end{aligned}
  $$
  \endgroup
  - Stratify by confounders $X=x$
  - ATE is the weighted average of in-group $X=x$ treated-vs-control differences,
    weighted by group size $\Pr(X=x)$

- @Assumption@: **(Positivity)**: conditional probability of treatment must be
  $0 < \Pr(T | X) < 1$
  - I.e., all groups of X must have both treated and control units

* Confounding Bias
- @Definition@: **confounding** happens when there is an _open_ _backdoor path_
  through which association flows non-causally
  - _Backdoor path_ means a path that enters the treatment variable through an
    incoming arrow
  - _Open_: the path is unblocked (e.g., by conditioning), actively channeling
    association
  - The result is that treatment and outcome share a common cause

- @Problem@: it's difficult to adjust for all common causes
  - Some causes are unknown
  - Known causes might be difficult to observe

- @Example@: _"Does new management training program increase employers'
  engagement?"_
  - Training is optional
    - Managers who are already doing a great job can attend the program
    - The managers that need the training the most, don't attend
  - When you measure engagement of the teams, engagement under managers who took
    the training is much higher
  - There is a common cause between treatment and outcome, so they move together
    regardless of a causal effect
  - The solution is control for manager's quality before taking the training

* Surrogate Confounding

- @Problem@: the confounder variable might be not be observable / measurable
  - E.g., you can't measure confounder "manager quality"

- @Solution@: measure variable that can be proxies for confounder
  - Measure causes of the variable (e.g., manager's tenure, education level) or
    effects (e.g., team's attrition, past performance)

* Selection Bias
- @Definition@: **selection bias** is introduced when the analysis conditions on
  a variable $X$ that is a:
  - Collider $A \rightarrow X \leftarrow B$
    - E.g.,  $SatScores \rightarrow Admission \leftarrow Extracurriculars$
  - Mediator $T \rightarrow X \rightarrow Y$
    - E.g., excluding drug patients who got a drug-caused side effect biases the
      estimated drug effect on mortality
  - Post-treatment variable $Y \rightarrow X$
    - E.g., studying performance predictors only among promoted employees, where
      promotion depends on performance

- @Caveat@: selection bias is not only about "bad sampling", e.g.,
    - Analyze only survivors (survivorship bias)
    - Analyze only adherent patients (adherence bias)
    - Self-selection 
  - In practice selection bias occurs when the treatment assignment itself
    depends on the confounder

- @Caveat@: Can occur even with randomization
  - E.g., randomizing $T$ only decouples $T$ from confounders,
    but if we condition on a post-treatment variable during analysis still
    introduces bias.

- @Solution@: Need to condition on variables driving selection

- @Problem@: We cannot always fully eliminate selection bias when:
    - Things causing selection are unmeasured/unmeasurable
    - Treatment or outcome causes selection directly
    - Unmeasured common cause between selection and outcome

* Selection Bias: Example
- A software company wants to estimate the impact of a new feature

- @Procedure@
  - Randomize rollout of the feature
    - E.g., 10% of the customers are randomly chosen to get the feature
  - You can't measure satisfaction directly
    - Send a survey to customers in the treated and in the control group, asking
      if you would recommend the product

- @Question@: is the difference in satisfaction due entirely to causal effect
  of new feature?

```graphviz
digraph G {
    rankdir=LR;
    node [shape=ellipse, style=filled, fillcolor=white, fontname="Arial"];

    RND [label="RND"];
    NewFeature [label="New feature"];
    CustomerSat [label="Customer satisfaction"];
    SurveyResult [label="Survey result"];
    Response [label="Response", fillcolor="#add8e6"];

    {rank=same; SurveyResult; Response}
    {rank=same; RND}
    {rank=same; NewFeature}
    {rank=same; CustomerSat}

    RND -> NewFeature;
    NewFeature -> CustomerSat;
    NewFeature -> Response;
    CustomerSat -> SurveyResult;
    CustomerSat -> Response;
}
```

- @Problem@: you measure the satisfaction only for the customers who responded
  to the survey (i.e., conditioning on responding to the survey)

* Selection Bias Through the Collider: Example
// TODO(ai_gp): Add a better picture
- Suppose:
  - Treatment $T$ affects hospitalization $H$
  - Disease severity $S$ also affects hospitalization $H$
    $$
    T \rightarrow H \leftarrow S
    $$
  - $H$ is a collider

- If we analyze only hospitalized patients (conditioning on $H$)
  - $T$ and $S$ become statistically associated
  - Open the path between $T$ and $S$
  - This can create a false treatment effect

* Selection Bias Through Mediator: Example
- Suppose:
  - Treatment $T$ affects blood pressure $M$
  - Blood pressure affects stroke risk $Y$
    $$
    T \rightarrow M \rightarrow Y
    $$

- If we adjust for $M$:
  - Part of the treatment effect is blocked
  - Estimated causal effect may be biased
